{
	title: 'Concise repository agent guidance',
	spec: 'AGENTS.md holds concise, sectioned guidance for Git, rebuildable tasks, spec writing, privacy, tests, minimal code, multiple agents sharing a checkout, orchestration, progress reports and inspectable UI. Preserve the rules in the AGENTS.md artifact, including commit trailers, task traceability, private-file exclusions and safety boundaries. Apply Strunk & White without weakening requirements. Git defaults to rebase and autostash. When multiple agents share a checkout, disable autostash, never stash others’ changes, and rebase only when that checkout is clean and no other agent is editing it. Explain that agents in the same checkout share files and the Git index even without using git worktree. Avoid overlapping work; use worktrees only with a compelling reason or when the user insists; claim a fix only once its commit sits in the checkout the Hal host runs from. Wrap prose at 72 columns, preserving Markdown headings, list indentation, code blocks and inline code spans. Keep wording edits separate from paragraph reflow for review.',
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'The old Git paragraph requested autostash while the shared-tree rule forbade stashing uncommitted work. Keep the requested defaults but require --no-autostash for shared worktrees, commit before rebasing, and wait for others’ uncommitted work. Removed repeated task-tracking instructions and illustrative report output, not their requirements.',
		'Correction to the preceding note: stash is intended for uncommitted work; the blanket ban was overbroad. The guidance distinguishes exclusive worktrees from shared ones: disable autostash in shared worktrees, never stash others’ changes, and rebase only with a clean, idle shared tree.',
